A||||3||firstfiero||oxidized paint...help!||||1||
Z||000000||firstfiero||12-25-2000||08:48 AM||||     I'm sure I'm not the only person with this problem...but either due from past neglect or just the heat from the engine i have oxidized paint. The white scratches off with my fingernail and you can see the red underneath but my buffer won't take it off. should I be using some sort of low grade sand paper to get to the good paint? like I said in my post about the bra I'd like to take care of these things to make the car look a little better so I don't have to throw money into a paint job right away. I want to put my money into the engine,suspension and a sticky set of tires first. I'm no where near poor but if I put the money into both right away my wife will make me live in my fiero...or the death machine as she puts it... instead of my nice warm house.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks for any help  86se||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||12-25-2000||11:45 AM||||Usually when there's that white film it means the clearcoat is shot.<P>You could try some 1500 or 2000 grit wet sandpaper to see if it removes the haze, then follow up with a polishing compound.<P>If your problem is restricted to one or two panels (decklid, hood, roof, etc) then you can paint just these. And they can be painted with spray cans (I've done it and it looks good).<P>Decklids, fenders and the hood can also be removed and resprayed easily. Biggest problem if you're a do-it-yourselfer is the weather right now. Man, can't wait until Spring.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||12-25-2000||07:37 PM||||I had a similar problem on the hood of my car.  I sanded the clearcoat with 1000 and wet/dry dandpaper.  Then I used a couple cans of clearcoat which I then sanded with 1500 and 2000 w/d paper followed by polishing compound.  Turned out nice.  Certainly looks better than it did when I started!!  Just be careful not to sand the color off.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||12-25-2000||08:12 PM||||You can get great results with the above mentioned methods.  1500 grit/with water.  Use lots of water.   I added another step and used rubbing compound first (coarser) and then polishing compound followed with wax.  I had a ridge of the clear coat wearing away on my fender, and now it is very smooth.  I recommend Meguiar's Mirror Glaze Diamond Cut Rubbing Compound, and Dual Action Cleaner/Polish.<P>I have had great results with all of thier products.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
